    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d895">

                <pb facs="1036" n="918" xml:id="pg918"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.11173 Barley/76</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-12-29"><hi rendition="#italic">December 29,
                        1928</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 28, 1929.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4205</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Embassy’s despatch
                    No. 4102 of November 13, 1928,<anchor n="54" xml:id="d895-f1"/> I have the honor
                    to inform the Department that according to the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reichsanzeiger</hi> of December 28, 1928, the ordinance of September 27,
                    1928, governing the importation of barley from the United States into Germany,
                    has been extended to February 28, 1929.</p>
                <p>A further report is being prepared on the future prospects of American barley in
                    the German market which will be forwarded to the Department shortly.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jacob Gould
                    Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
